{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1229 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "772cec3e4d94e662d80274ccb0ea6bd6370f95f1e31b78c6b4c0646ca77a51ed" :edb/db-before-t 1228 :edb/db-after-t 1229 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534541 50 #inst "2026-10-07T21:28:38.342Z" 13194139534541 true] [17592186063964 1100 "Find the $x$-values of the stationary points of $f(x) =  x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2$ and classify each point." 13194139534541 true] [17592186063964 1100 "Find the $x$-values of the stationary points of $f(x) = x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2$ and classify each point." 13194139534541 false] [17592186063964 1145 "First, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 4x^3 - 16 x\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{3} - 16x &= 0 \\\\\nx^{3} - 4x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 4) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x + 2)(x - 2) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=\\pm 2$.\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of $f''(x)$ at the critical points. Computing $f''(x)$, we get\n\n$$\nf''(x)= 12 x^2 - 16\n$$\n\nand evaluating $f''(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(-2) &= 12(-2)^{2} - 16 = 32 > 0 \\\\\nf^{″}(0) &= 12(0)^{2} - 16 =-16 < 0 \\\\\nf^{″}(2) &= 12(2)^{2} - 16 = 32 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=0$, so this is a relative maximum. On the other hand, the second derivative is positive at $x=\\pm 2$, so these are relative minima." 13194139534541 false] [17592186063964 1145 "First, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x) = x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x)  = 4x^3 - 16 x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-16x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-4x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ x(x+2)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=\\pm 2.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of $f''(x)$ at the critical points. Computing $f''(x),$ we get\n\n$$\nf''(x)= 12 x^2 - 16,\n$$\n\nand evaluating $f''(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =12(-2)^{2}-16=32>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)^{2}-16=-16<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =12(2)^{2}-16=32>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=0,$ so this is a relative maximum. On the other hand, the second derivative is positive at $x=\\pm 2,$ so these are relative minima." 13194139534541 true] [17592186063965 1100 "Consider the function $f(x) = x^3-6x^2-15x$. Given that $f'(5) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f''(5) > 0$\n2. $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5$\n3. $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=5$" 13194139534541 false] [17592186063965 1100 "Consider the function $f(x) = x^3-6x^2-15x.$ Given that $f'(5) = 0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f''(5)  >           0$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=5$" 13194139534541 true] [17592186063965 1145 "We're given that $x=5$ is a stationary point of $f(x)$. We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)= x^3-6x^2-15x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 3x^2-12x-15\n$$\n\nWe're given that $f'(5) = 0$. Indeed, we can see that\n\n$$\nf'(5) = 3(5)^2 - 12(5) - 15 = 0\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x) &= 6x - 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(5) &= 6(5) - 12 \\\\\n&= 30 - 12 \\\\\n&= 18 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534541 false] [17592186063965 1145 "We're given that $x=5$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=  x^3-6x^2-15x,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) =  3x^2-12x-15\n$$\n\nWe're given that $f'(5) = 0.$ Indeed, we can see that $f'(5) = 3(5)^2 - 12(5) - 15 = 0.$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =6x-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(5) & =6(5)-12 \\\\  & =30-12 \\\\  & =18>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534541 true] [17592186064081 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293827 13194139534541 true] [17592186064081 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293834 13194139534541 true] [17592186064081 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293869 13194139534541 true] [17592186064081 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293883 13194139534541 true] [17592186064082 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293841 13194139534541 true] [17592186064082 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293848 13194139534541 true] [17592186064082 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293855 13194139534541 true] [17592186064082 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293862 13194139534541 true] [17592186064082 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293876 13194139534541 true] [17592186064555 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534541 true] [17592186064555 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFor the function $f(x) = 6x^{2} - 4x^{3}$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f^{″}(0) > 0$\n2. $x = 0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n3. $f^{″}(1) < 0$\n4. $x = 1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$" 13194139534541 false] [17592186064555 1100 "For the function $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- $x=0$ is a relative minimum of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $x=1$ is a relative maximum of $f(x)$" 13194139534541 true] [17592186064555 1145 "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we then need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12x-12{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 12x(1-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,1.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12-24x,\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-12<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so this is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III and VI are true. The second derivative is negative at $x=1,$ so this is a relative maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I, II, III, and IV.\"" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293827 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293828 13194139534541 true] [17592186293827 1089 "q-114414" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293827 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534541 true] [17592186293827 1100 "For the function $f(x)=x-{e}^{x},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=0$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{″}(0)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293827 1113 #uuid "9c703bb5-c256-594b-9d43-e00104658951" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293827 1145 "We need to identify whether $x=0$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=x-{e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have a critical point at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=-{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(0)=-1<0.\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=0,$ so this critical point is a relative *maximum*. \n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=0$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,✓$\n\n- ${f}^{″}(0)<0\\,✓$\n\n- $f(0)$ has a relative *maximum* at $x=0\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293828 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293829 13194139534541 true] [17592186293828 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293830 13194139534541 true] [17592186293828 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293831 13194139534541 true] [17592186293828 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293832 13194139534541 true] [17592186293828 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293833 13194139534541 true] [17592186293828 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534541 true] [17592186293828 1041 "selection" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293828 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293832 13194139534541 true] [17592186293828 1110 #uuid "495bd8d2-b59c-5fff-81a2-2e8195513d9a" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293829 1034 "None of the statements are true" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293829 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534541 true] [17592186293829 1111 #uuid "0efa54de-aaac-5dff-9339-d60a5ec4bbf4" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293830 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293830 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534541 true] [17592186293830 1111 #uuid "976d869b-eb2d-50d8-a0c1-d9a57a688a56" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293831 1034 "I only" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293831 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534541 true] [17592186293831 1111 #uuid "2de86e8b-0bf7-52d2-b487-a882601ff09f" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293832 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293832 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534541 true] [17592186293832 1111 #uuid "79d6c248-2d4a-59f5-a62c-a8828d4548b7" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293833 1034 "II only" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293833 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534541 true] [17592186293833 1111 #uuid "fd0e7ba3-d268-5081-84a2-16d15be885eb" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293834 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293835 13194139534541 true] [17592186293834 1089 "q-114415" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293834 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534541 true] [17592186293834 1100 "For the function $f(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2}){e}^{x},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$\n- ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-1$" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293834 1113 #uuid "625bbe54-0b3f-5afb-b15e-eb3a5b309cd0" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293834 1145 "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2}){e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1+2x){e}^{x}=({x}^{2}+x){e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+x){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=-1,0.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=({x}^{2}+x)\\cdot {e}^{x}+(2x+1){e}^{x}=({x}^{2}+3x+1){e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =-{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so there is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III is false. $x=1$ is not a critical point.\n\n- Statement IV is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only.\"" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293835 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293836 13194139534541 true] [17592186293835 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293837 13194139534541 true] [17592186293835 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293838 13194139534541 true] [17592186293835 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293839 13194139534541 true] [17592186293835 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293840 13194139534541 true] [17592186293835 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534541 true] [17592186293835 1041 "selection" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293835 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293836 13194139534541 true] [17592186293835 1110 #uuid "f29de4f2-d3d7-5c89-9a1e-6f0bd70b60fe" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293836 1034 "I, II, and IV only" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293836 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534541 true] [17592186293836 1111 #uuid "0a38dba5-622e-5356-bae0-ac479825ebd3" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293837 1034 "III only" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293837 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534541 true] [17592186293837 1111 #uuid "be64c842-1c2a-5e23-a999-aa07cfad96f4" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293838 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293838 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534541 true] [17592186293838 1111 #uuid "5c808dd9-e862-5d77-b630-69b56e7db2f8" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293839 1034 "I, II, and III only" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293839 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534541 true] [17592186293839 1111 #uuid "381f926c-73d2-55a8-b374-e171f87db6f4" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293840 1034 "II only" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293840 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534541 true] [17592186293840 1111 #uuid "7cf16a51-e154-5a7d-95d8-b1d2a62fdb49" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293841 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293842 13194139534541 true] [17592186293841 1089 "q-21536" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293841 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534541 true] [17592186293841 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+8.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(1)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293841 1113 #uuid "294cdcbe-5050-50a2-a47c-f7aeafc041f8" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293841 1145 "We're given that $x=1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+8,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+2x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12{x}^{2}-12x+2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =12(1)^{2}-12(1)+2 \\\\  & =2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293843 13194139534541 true] [17592186293842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293844 13194139534541 true] [17592186293842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293845 13194139534541 true] [17592186293842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293846 13194139534541 true] [17592186293842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293847 13194139534541 true] [17592186293842 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534541 true] [17592186293842 1041 "selection" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293842 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293847 13194139534541 true] [17592186293842 1110 #uuid "8b6f0d46-1560-5013-8ce9-928e6a66d630" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293843 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293843 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534541 true] [17592186293843 1111 #uuid "7799333c-5cab-556b-8064-b1a628ef6985" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293844 1034 "I only" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293844 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534541 true] [17592186293844 1111 #uuid "ae68b24d-8c35-5402-a0ac-ff5ccea754bd" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293845 1034 "III only" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293845 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534541 true] [17592186293845 1111 #uuid "67e6f828-2dd2-5df9-9b1e-e3a4747b4e65" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293846 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534541 true] [17592186293846 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534541 true] [1759218